{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-942",
              "name": "Reflected credentialed CORS allows cross-origin reads of authenticated admin data",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Reflected credentialed CORS allows cross-origin reads of authenticated admin data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The API reflects arbitrary `Origin` values while enabling credentials, allowing a malicious website to read responses from authenticated endpoints when a user is logged in. This permits cross-origin disclosure of administrative venue data."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The API reflects arbitrary `Origin` values while enabling credentials, allowing a malicious website to read responses from authenticated endpoints when a user is logged in. This permits cross-origin disclosure of administrative venue data.\n\nAn attacker can host a page on an arbitrary origin and induce an authenticated administrator or staff user to visit it. The browser sends the `gioi_session` cookie and exposes API responses to attacker-controlled JavaScript, allowing unauthorized reading of data returned by `/admin/venues` and potentially other credentialed endpoints.\n\nConfigure CORS with an explicit allowlist of trusted production origins and reject all other origins. Never reflect the request `Origin` when credentials are enabled. Apply the same policy consistently to preflight and actual responses, and avoid enabling credentialed CORS on administrative endpoints unless it is strictly required.",
                "markdown": "The API reflects arbitrary `Origin` values while enabling credentials, allowing a malicious website to read responses from authenticated endpoints when a user is logged in. This permits cross-origin disclosure of administrative venue data.\n\nAn attacker can host a page on an arbitrary origin and induce an authenticated administrator or staff user to visit it. The browser sends the `gioi_session` cookie and exposes API responses to attacker-controlled JavaScript, allowing unauthorized reading of data returned by `/admin/venues` and potentially other credentialed endpoints.\n\nConfigure CORS with an explicit allowlist of trusted production origins and reject all other origins. Never reflect the request `Origin` when credentials are enabled. Apply the same policy consistently to preflight and actual responses, and avoid enabling credentialed CORS on administrative endpoints unless it is strictly required."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-942",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/942.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-200",
              "name": "Unauthenticated Nuxt development server exposes application source and Vite internals",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated Nuxt development server exposes application source and Vite internals"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The frontend on `http://host.docker.internal:3002` is running in Nuxt development mode and serves application source modules and Vite development assets to unauthenticated clients. Attackers can retrieve route, authentication, API, and business-logic source code, along with absolute developer filesystem paths."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The frontend on `http://host.docker.internal:3002` is running in Nuxt development mode and serves application source modules and Vite development assets to unauthenticated clients. Attackers can retrieve route, authentication, API, and business-logic source code, along with absolute developer filesystem paths.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker can download internal frontend source such as `composables/useApi.ts` and `stores/auth.ts`, enumerate privileged API operations and client-side authorization assumptions, and retrieve the Vite runtime. The disclosure materially lowers the effort required to target the separate API, identify hidden administrative workflows, and chain authorization or object-access weaknesses. Absolute local paths and dependency versions also disclose deployment details.\n\nRun the Nuxt frontend in a production build (`nuxt build` followed by the production server) for any exposed environment. Bind the Vite/Nuxt development server only to a local interface or a protected development network, and require authentication at the reverse proxy if development access is unavoidable. Disable public access to Vite source modules, `@fs` paths, `@vite/client`, source maps, and HMR endpoints. Review deployment automation so `dev` mode cannot be started in production.",
                "markdown": "The frontend on `http://host.docker.internal:3002` is running in Nuxt development mode and serves application source modules and Vite development assets to unauthenticated clients. Attackers can retrieve route, authentication, API, and business-logic source code, along with absolute developer filesystem paths.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker can download internal frontend source such as `composables/useApi.ts` and `stores/auth.ts`, enumerate privileged API operations and client-side authorization assumptions, and retrieve the Vite runtime. The disclosure materially lowers the effort required to target the separate API, identify hidden administrative workflows, and chain authorization or object-access weaknesses. Absolute local paths and dependency versions also disclose deployment details.\n\nRun the Nuxt frontend in a production build (`nuxt build` followed by the production server) for any exposed environment. Bind the Vite/Nuxt development server only to a local interface or a protected development network, and require authentication at the reverse proxy if development access is unavoidable. Disable public access to Vite source modules, `@fs` paths, `@vite/client`, source maps, and HMR endpoints. Review deployment automation so `dev` mode cannot be started in production."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-1236",
              "name": "CSV Formula Injection in Admin Venue Reservation Export",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CSV Formula Injection in Admin Venue Reservation Export"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The public reservation endpoint accepts guest names beginning with spreadsheet formula syntax, and the admin venue CSV export emits that value without neutralization. An administrator opening the downloaded CSV in a spreadsheet can trigger the injected formula, enabling phishing or outbound navigation from the administrative workstation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The public reservation endpoint accepts guest names beginning with spreadsheet formula syntax, and the admin venue CSV export emits that value without neutralization. An administrator opening the downloaded CSV in a spreadsheet can trigger the injected formula, enabling phishing or outbound navigation from the administrative workstation.\n\nAn unauthenticated user can persist a formula in a reservation and have it delivered to administrators through `/admin/venues/{venueId}/export.csv`. When opened by a spreadsheet application, the `HYPERLINK` formula is interpreted rather than treated as text, allowing attacker-controlled navigation and potentially other spreadsheet actions depending on the client and formula payload.\n\nNeutralize spreadsheet formula prefixes in every exported cell derived from user-controlled data. Before CSV serialization, prefix values whose first non- whitespace character is one of `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@` with a single quote (or use an equivalent proven CSV-injection mitigation), while preserving CSV quoting. Apply the same policy to all export fields and add regression tests covering formula prefixes and leading whitespace.",
                "markdown": "The public reservation endpoint accepts guest names beginning with spreadsheet formula syntax, and the admin venue CSV export emits that value without neutralization. An administrator opening the downloaded CSV in a spreadsheet can trigger the injected formula, enabling phishing or outbound navigation from the administrative workstation.\n\nAn unauthenticated user can persist a formula in a reservation and have it delivered to administrators through `/admin/venues/{venueId}/export.csv`. When opened by a spreadsheet application, the `HYPERLINK` formula is interpreted rather than treated as text, allowing attacker-controlled navigation and potentially other spreadsheet actions depending on the client and formula payload.\n\nNeutralize spreadsheet formula prefixes in every exported cell derived from user-controlled data. Before CSV serialization, prefix values whose first non- whitespace character is one of `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@` with a single quote (or use an equivalent proven CSV-injection mitigation), while preserving CSV quoting. Apply the same policy to all export fields and add regression tests covering formula prefixes and leading whitespace."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-1236",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1236.html"
            },
